== 17. Your Relations to the Lord Call for Secret Prayer

Consider that the near and dear relation in which you#super[+] stand to the Lord calls aloud for secret prayer (John 15:14–15). You#super[+] are His friends. Now, a true friend loves to visit his friend when he may find him alone and enjoy privacy with him. A true friend loves to pour out his heart into the bosom of his friend when he has him in a corner, or in the field, or under a hedge. You#super[+] are His favorites. And what favorite is there who hides his secret from his prince? Do not all favorites open their hearts to their princes when they are alone? You#super[+] are His children. And what true-hearted child is there who does not delight to be much with his father when he is alone, when nobody is by? Oh, how free and open children are when they have their parents alone, beyond what they are when company is present! You#super[+] are the spouse of Christ. And what spouse, what wife, is there who does not love to be much with her husband when he is alone? True lovers are always at their best when they are most alone. “I belong to my beloved, and his desire is for me. Come, my beloved, let us go out to the fields; let us spend the night among the wildflowers. Let us go early to the vineyards to see if the vine has budded, if the blossom has opened, if the pomegranates are in bloom—there I will give you my love” (Song 7:10–12). The spouse of Christ is very eager to enjoy His company in the fields, so that, having her beloved alone, she might open her heart to Him more freely and more secretly. As wives, when they are walking alone with their husbands in the fields, are freer to open their minds and the secrets of their hearts than they are in their houses with their children and servants about them, so it was with the spouse.

Beyond all doubt, those who seldom or never converse with Christ in their closets, who are shy of Christ when they are alone, who never make a habit of paying Christ secret visits, have very great reason to question whether they are Christ’s real friends, favorites, children, spouse. Christ may say to very many in our days what Delilah said to Samson: “How can you say, ‘I love you,’ when you have failed to reveal to me the source of your great strength?” (Judg 16:15)—the revealing of which secret at last cost him his life. How can you#super[+] say you#super[+] love Me when you#super[+] never acquaint Me with your#super[+] secrets? How can you#super[+] say you#super[+] love Me when you#super[+] never pay Me any private visits? How can you#super[+] say that you#super[+] are My friends, My faithful friends, My bosom friends, when you#super[+] never unbosom yourselves#super[+] to Me in private? How can you#super[+] say that you#super[+] are My favorites, when you#super[+] can spend one month after another, and one quarter of a year after another, and yet not let Me know one of all your#super[+] secrets, when every day you#super[+] might have My ear in secret if you#super[+] pleased? How can you#super[+] say that you#super[+] are My children, and yet be as secretive and reserved as you#super[+] are? How can you#super[+] say that you#super[+] are My spouse, and that you#super[+] lie in My bosom, and yet never take any delight in opening your#super[+] hearts, your#super[+] secrets, to Me when I am alone? What Alexander said to a man who bore his name but was a coward, “Either lay down the name of Alexander, or fight like Alexander,” I say to you#super[+]. Either be frequent in closet duties, as befits a Christian, or else lay down the name of a Christian. Either unbosom yourselves#super[+] in secret to Christ as friends, favorites, children, spouses, or else lay down these names.
